Where the Hershey data in this directory came from.

The Usenet distribution of the Hershey fonts (comp.sources.unix volume 4, formatted by James
Hurt) is carried verbatim, as .jhf files, by the Debian and Ubuntu package hershey-fonts-data.
The two files here were taken from that package and are not modified (cmp-identical):

  Package   hershey-fonts-data 0.1-1.1build1 (Ubuntu 24.04 "noble", universe, all architectures)
  Archive   http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/ (apt-get download hershey-fonts-data)
  Package   SHA-256 dd9288d141a62c2c92bcf055edf907f41c365e9d8e529e8ada469a16cc9e3cc1
  Upstream  http://www.whence.com/hershey-fonts/ (packaging by Kamal Mostafa), whose copyright
            file lists the data as "Copyright: 1967 Dr. A. V. Hershey, James Hurt", "downloaded
            from http://emergent.unpythonic.net/software/hershey"

  This file       Package path                          SHA-256
  romans.jhf      /usr/share/hershey-fonts/rowmans.jhf  8718fb129c0f6bce89c84fe41bc467e39534d215a6f7c3220cc5789a8a7d8618
                  (Roman Simplex)
  romant.jhf      /usr/share/hershey-fonts/rowmant.jhf  8b280fa0c63aa003a80cbb5bbe4ef2535e0b61ca464d3b8e6e634ce0881b9b67
                  (Roman Triplex)

The package spells the file names rowmans and rowmant; they are saved here as romans and romant,
the names the Usenet distribution gives them. Only the names differ.

NOTICE holds the distribution's notice. It is the text of the "License: hershey-license" paragraph
of the package's /usr/share/doc/hershey-fonts-data/copyright, with the single space that the
Debian copyright format puts in front of each line of a multi-line field removed and nothing else
changed. The terms require it to be distributed with the font data, and forbid converting the
data to the format distributed by the U.S. NTIS, so the data stays in .jhf form here.

Each file holds 96 records, one per line. The first 95 are the printable ASCII characters 32 to
126 in order; the 96th is an extra ring glyph that src/hershey.rs does not load.

One record is odd: in romant.jhf the glyph for J (number 3010) has the bounds pair "JJ" (-8 to -8)
around ink from -6 to 6. The file is left as it is; src/hershey.rs gives a record whose bounds miss
its ink the ink's extent plus 2 units either side, which makes J -8 to 8.
